WEBVTT

00:00:04.713 --> 00:00:06.757
Holy buckets.

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:10.469
Eat it.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:11.303
Go on.

00:00:11.345 --> 00:00:12.429
It’ll be delicious.

00:00:12.471 --> 00:00:13.847
It’s the same bar,

00:00:13.931 --> 00:00:15.974
it’s just gotten a little
smaller on the journey,

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:17.809
that’s all.

00:00:17.893 --> 00:00:19.895
( teeth clicking )

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.693
It’s great.

00:00:26.777 --> 00:00:28.946
It’s a miracle.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.824
So imagine you’re sitting
at home, watching television,

00:00:32.908 --> 00:00:34.493
and suddenly
a commercial

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:35.827
will flash onto the screen.

00:00:35.911 --> 00:00:37.162
A voice will say:

00:00:37.246 --> 00:00:39.957
Wonka’s chocolates
are the best

00:00:39.998 --> 00:00:41.917
in the world.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.670
If you don’t believe us,
try one for yourself.

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:50.008
And you simply reach out
and take it.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.176
How about that?

00:00:51.218 --> 00:00:52.344
So can you send
other things?

00:00:52.386 --> 00:00:55.514
Say, like,
breakfast cereal?

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:58.267
Do you have any idea what
breakfast cereal’s made of?

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.185
It’s those little
curly wooden shavings

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:01.687
you find in
pencil sharpeners.

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.397
But could you send it
by television,

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.439
if you wanted to?

00:01:04.523 --> 00:01:05.732
’Course I could.

00:01:05.816 --> 00:01:06.733
What about people?

00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:08.610
Well, why would I
want to send a person?

00:01:08.694 --> 00:01:09.987
They don’t taste
very good at all.

00:01:10.028 --> 00:01:12.948
Don’t realize
what you’ve invented?

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:14.116
It’s a teleporter.

00:01:14.199 --> 00:01:15.242
It’s the most important
invention

00:01:15.325 --> 00:01:16.702
in the history
of the world.

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:18.704
And all you think about
is chocolate.

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:21.373
Calm down, Mike.

00:01:21.415 --> 00:01:23.417
I think Mr. Wonka knows
what he’s talking about.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.502
No, he doesn’t.
He has no idea.

00:01:25.544 --> 00:01:28.755
You think he’s a genius,
but he’s an idiot.

00:01:28.839 --> 00:01:31.633
But I’m not.

00:01:36.013 --> 00:01:37.055
Hey, little boy...

00:01:38.724 --> 00:01:40.726
Don’t push my button.

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:46.982
( machine whirs )

00:01:54.698 --> 00:01:58.368
( high-pitched,
oscillating tones )

00:02:08.587 --> 00:02:10.380
( ominous music builds )

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.597
( zapping, crackling )

00:02:21.433 --> 00:02:22.726
He’s gone.

00:02:22.768 --> 00:02:25.479
Let’s go check the television,
see what we get.

00:02:26.563 --> 00:02:29.483
I sure hope no part of him
gets left behind.

00:02:29.566 --> 00:02:30.734
What do you mean?

00:02:30.776 --> 00:02:33.320
Well, sometimes only
half the little pieces

00:02:33.403 --> 00:02:34.863
find their way through.

00:02:36.448 --> 00:02:39.201
If you had to choose
only one half of your son,

00:02:39.284 --> 00:02:40.285
which one would it be?

00:02:40.369 --> 00:02:42.454
What kind of a question is that?

00:02:42.537 --> 00:02:44.956
No need to snap.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.042
Just a question.

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:48.710
Try every channel.

00:02:48.794 --> 00:02:50.962
I’m starting to feel
a little anxious.

00:02:52.547 --> 00:02:56.093
( pulsing rock music plays )

00:02:56.134 --> 00:02:57.052
There he is.

00:02:57.135 --> 00:02:58.178
Mike.

00:02:58.261 --> 00:03:00.347
♪ The most important thing ♪

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:01.807
♪ That we’ve ever learned ♪

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:03.642
♪ The most important thing
we’ve learned ♪

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:05.185
♪ As far as children
are concerned ♪

00:03:05.268 --> 00:03:07.813
♪ Is never to do we let them
near the television set ♪

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:10.482
♪ Or better still,
just don’t install ♪

00:03:10.565 --> 00:03:12.192
♪ The idiotic thing at all ♪

00:03:12.275 --> 00:03:13.860
♪ Ah-ah, never, never let them ♪

00:03:13.944 --> 00:03:15.779
♪ Ah-ah,
never, never let them ♪

00:03:15.821 --> 00:03:17.823
♪ Ah-ah, ah-ah, ah-ah, ah-ah ♪

00:03:17.906 --> 00:03:19.116
( hard rock music plays )

00:03:19.157 --> 00:03:22.327
♪ Oh-oh-oh-oh-oh-oh-oh... ♪

00:03:22.411 --> 00:03:25.705
( bass guitar, drums play )

00:03:28.583 --> 00:03:30.502
♪ Never, never let them ♪

00:03:30.544 --> 00:03:31.920
♪ Never,
never let them ♪

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:33.588
♪ It rots the senses
in the head ♪

00:03:33.672 --> 00:03:35.006
♪ It keeps imagination dead ♪

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:36.842
♪ It clogs and clutters up
the mind ♪

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:38.677
♪ It makes a child
so dull and blind ♪

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:40.262
♪ So dull, so dull ♪

00:03:40.345 --> 00:03:43.390
♪ You can no longer understand
a fairy tale in fairyland ♪

00:03:43.473 --> 00:03:45.058
♪ In fairyland, in fairyland ♪

00:03:45.142 --> 00:03:46.643
♪ His brain becomes
as soft as cheese ♪

00:03:46.685 --> 00:03:48.520
♪ His thinking power
starts to freeze ♪

00:03:48.603 --> 00:03:51.857
♪ He cannot think,
he only sees ♪

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:59.531
♪ Regarding little Mike Teavee ♪

00:03:59.614 --> 00:04:02.367
♪ We very much regret that we ♪

00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:04.703
♪ Regret that we ♪

00:04:05.954 --> 00:04:07.456
♪ Shall simply have
to wait and see ♪

00:04:07.539 --> 00:04:10.125
♪ And see, and see, and see ♪

00:04:10.208 --> 00:04:11.668
♪ And see, and see ♪

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:12.961
♪ We very much regret that we ♪

00:04:13.044 --> 00:04:15.046
♪ Shall simply have to wait
and see ♪

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:19.801
♪ If we can get him back
to shape, but if we can’t... ♪

00:04:19.885 --> 00:04:21.052
♪ It serves him right. ♪

00:04:21.136 --> 00:04:22.721
Ew! Somebody grab him.

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:25.140
( high-pitched voice ):
Help me.

00:04:25.223 --> 00:04:26.892
Help me.

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.227
( flatly ):
Oh, thank heavens.

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:31.021
He’s completely unharmed.

00:04:31.062 --> 00:04:32.147
Unharmed?

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:34.524
What are you talking about?

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:39.237
Just put me back in
the other way.

00:04:39.321 --> 00:04:41.573
There is no other way.

00:04:41.656 --> 00:04:43.200
It’s television, not telephone.

00:04:43.241 --> 00:04:44.534
There’s quite a difference.

00:04:44.576 --> 00:04:47.162
And what exactly do you
propose to do about it?

00:04:47.245 --> 00:04:49.581
I don’t know.

00:04:49.623 --> 00:04:50.832
But young men

00:04:50.916 --> 00:04:53.293
are extremely springy;
they stretch like mad.

00:04:53.376 --> 00:04:54.503
( gasps )

00:04:54.586 --> 00:04:56.296
Let’s go put him
in the taffy puller.

00:04:56.379 --> 00:04:58.006
Taffy puller?!

00:04:58.089 --> 00:04:59.925
Hey, that was my idea.

00:05:01.843 --> 00:05:04.262
Boy, is he going to be skinny.

00:05:04.346 --> 00:05:05.931
WONKA:
Yeah.

00:05:06.014 --> 00:05:08.725
Taffy puller.

00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:12.729
I want you to take Mr. Teavee
and his...

00:05:12.771 --> 00:05:16.024
little boy up to
the taffy puller, okay?

00:05:16.107 --> 00:05:18.610
Stretch him out.

00:05:23.198 --> 00:05:24.866
( gasps )

00:05:25.909 --> 00:05:28.245
Whew!

00:05:28.286 --> 00:05:29.746
On with the tour.

00:05:31.289 --> 00:05:33.041
( computer powers down )

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:41.675
There’s still
so much left to see.

00:05:41.758 --> 00:05:44.678
Now, how many children are left?

00:05:46.888 --> 00:05:51.810
Mr. Wonka, Charlie’s
the only one left now.

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:55.397
You mean you’re the only one?

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.440
( whispers ):
Yes, sir.

00:05:57.482 --> 00:05:59.818
What happened to the others?

00:06:03.738 --> 00:06:04.823
Oh, my dear boy.

00:06:04.906 --> 00:06:05.991
Well, that means you’ve won.

00:06:06.074 --> 00:06:08.827
Oh, I do congratulate you,
I really do.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.203
I’m absolutely delighted.

00:06:10.287 --> 00:06:12.247
I had a hunch, you know,
right from the beginning.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:13.373
Well done.

00:06:13.456 --> 00:06:15.250
Now we mustn’t
dilly or dally.

00:06:15.333 --> 00:06:17.252
Because we have an enormous
number of things to do

00:06:17.335 --> 00:06:19.087
before the day’s out.
But luckily for us,

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:20.505
we have the great
glass elevator

00:06:20.547 --> 00:06:22.090
to speed things al...
( thud )

00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:30.432
Speed things along.

00:06:30.515 --> 00:06:32.350
( bell dings )

00:06:32.434 --> 00:06:34.686
Come on.

00:06:39.482 --> 00:06:40.650
( bell dings )

00:06:40.692 --> 00:06:41.776
"Up and out"?

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:43.445
What kind of a room is that?

00:06:43.528 --> 00:06:44.863
Hold on.

00:06:44.904 --> 00:06:46.281
( rumbling )

00:06:54.205 --> 00:06:55.206
Oh, my goodness.

00:06:55.248 --> 00:06:57.292
We’re going to need
to go much faster.

00:06:57.375 --> 00:06:59.711
Otherwise, we’ll just
never break through.

00:06:59.794 --> 00:07:01.129
Break through what?

00:07:01.212 --> 00:07:04.341
I’ve been longing to press
that button for years.

00:07:04.382 --> 00:07:06.718
Well, here we go.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:07.719
Up and out.

00:07:07.761 --> 00:07:09.512
But do you really mean...?

00:07:09.554 --> 00:07:10.513
Yeah, I do.

00:07:10.555 --> 00:07:11.973
But it’s made of glass.

00:07:12.057 --> 00:07:14.309
It’ll smash into
a million pieces.

00:07:14.392 --> 00:07:16.728
( giggling )

00:07:26.029 --> 00:07:27.864
( dramatic music plays )

